92186512861 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512861 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512861 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe515d97-b46e-5b25-875a-5d4729fa788a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffa36a73-cb38-5a3e-b22f-94ae7972e4d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0821d791-2798-561e-8237-7b4aabdd155c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "124cf999-fb4b-5e99-b208-917f67eaa2a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=6.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))=-2(6)=-12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}=3(1)^{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{3} \\\\  & =-4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-293604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1071 "A generator consumes $3$ liters of fuel per hour for the first two hours of use and $5$ liters for each additional hour. Which function $F$ gives the total amount of fuel consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18899ab8-1919-5955-a40e-8e20033f8007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512876 #edb/ref 1074 "The total amount of fuel consumed consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $2$ hours, the generator consumes\n\n$$\n3\\times 2=6\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\n- For each additional hour beyond the first $2,$ the generator consumes\n\n$$\n5(t-2)\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total amount of fuel $F$ consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2,$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =6+5(t-2) \\\\  & =6+5t-10 \\\\  & =5t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1069 "q-288661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\n(ax-8)(x+b)=4{x}^{2}-32x+48\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$a+b=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19e31d5d-5933-52fe-a699-d3240dcd05a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512877 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(ax-8)(x+b) & =a{x}^{2}+abx-8x-8b \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b=4{x}^{2}-32x+48.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\na=4.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n-8\\cdot b=48⇒b=-6.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $a=4$ and $b=-6$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ab-8 & =4\\cdot (-6)-8 \\\\  & =-24-8 \\\\  & =-32\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $a+b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =4+(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=a{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+bx+1$ has a factor of $(x-1)$ and has a remainder of $4$ when divided by $(x+2).$ Find the value of $a-b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5db961df-fac4-59ee-a59e-6595583c0931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(1) \\\\  & =a(1)^{3}-2(1)^{2}+b(1)+1 \\\\  & =a-2+b+1 \\\\  & =a+b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+1 \\\\  & =-8a-8-2b+1 \\\\  & =-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=a+b-1 \\\\ 4=-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+b=1 \\\\ 8a+2b=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ and we add the resulting equation to the second equation. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2a-2b=-2 \\\\ +\\,\\,\\, & \\,8a+2b=-11 \\\\  & \\,6a=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=-\\frac{13}{6}.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a+b=1$ to find $b=\\frac{19}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $a-b=-\\frac{16}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f3a9a12-37bd-5534-bce9-24153ef1f5e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{e}={e}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1071 "Caden sold half of his comic books and then bought sixteen more. If he now has $36$, how many comic books did he have at the start?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "671acf01-53e4-5ebe-ba29-cc75ccac1d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x=$ the number of comic books Caden had at the start.\n\nWe note that:\n\n- Half the number of comic books corresponds to $\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half of them corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half and buying sixteen more corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16.$\n\nThe final number of comic books is $36,$ so we have the equation\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{1}{2}x+16=36.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2})x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16-16 & =36-16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =20 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\ x & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Caden had $40$ comic books at the start." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1071 "A company rents ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular prism. Each unit is $5\\,\\text{yd}$ long, $3\\,\\text{yd}$ wide, and $3\\text{yd}$ high. If it costs $$2$ per cubic yard per day to rent a storage unit, how much does it cost the company each day to rent the storage units?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "751e0aa2-a931-559b-9a2f-5250360d1346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5,\\,w=3,\\,h=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=5\\cdot 3\\cdot 3=45\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the ten units is\n\n$$\n45\\cdot 10=450\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to rent the storage units, each day the company must pay\n\n$$\n450\\cdot $2=$900.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23f0dba237502bc7261fd8f28327beefd5872bb4b0f503930c488b2857fcef24.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e50efe9-568f-54f5-835e-03a6e09d3518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/d1/d14f62179f831ebdc177eac7bd2899679864305d4104d4b61f304eb4fa9e133d.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={210}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $a:$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+4b=7 \\\\ -2a+6b=20\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b8bf0b2-6f55-5107-9e2d-cc11b6142890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5359436-369e-5935-88c6-73a8053a9364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1071 "A team of $5$ players is to be chosen at random from $7$ boys and $5$ girls.  What is the probability that $2$ boys and $3$ girls will be chosen? Round your answer to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7f9805c-ce96-515d-891a-332594997042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ and $n$ is the number of picks where $2$ are boys and $3$ are girls.\n\nSince the order in which the players are chosen does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $5$ people from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{12}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!}=792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $7$) boys is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{2}=\\frac{7!}{2!\\cdot 5!}=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $5$) girls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{5}{C}_{3}=21\\cdot 10=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{210}{792}≈0.27=27%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
